cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this report or its code, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Why item-level DIF tests have low power in small samples, and what it takes to raise it: MIMIC, IRT likelihood-ratio and Rasch-tree tests for binary items"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-22
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/wangqiu-syr/dif-power-binary-items"
abstract: >-
  A report, with code and results, on the power of item-level differential item functioning (DIF)
  tests for binary items in small samples. Simulation 1 (10 Rasch items, 50 examinees per group,
  1,000 replications) compares MIMIC, the IRT likelihood-ratio test (difR::difLRT) and the Rasch
  tree; exact calculations show why power is low; a pre-specified simulation (40 conditions x 300
  replications) tests what raises it.
keywords:
  - differential item functioning
  - statistical power
  - MIMIC
  - likelihood-ratio test
  - Rasch tree
  - Mantel-Haenszel
  - simulation study
